Chapter: ProbeShake
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The tree-shaking closure, with zero mention of zig, and the seven fixtures
 that settle it.

 The algorithm is reachability in a directed graph plus "keep the input
 order". Roots name parts; parts name other parts. Everything target-specific
 -- where roots come from, what the text means, how it is concatenated back --
 lives in the caller and is deliberately absent here.

 This file exists so the algorithm can be proven BEFORE ZigEmitter is opened.
 It is also a tier, so it runs on bare metal and through the plug and the two
 must agree: the closure itself gets the double-compiling treatment.

 THE ORDER RULE IS LOAD-BEARING. `kept` comes back in the parts table's
 original order, never topologically sorted. Container-level declarations do
 not care about order -- PR 95 proved that by moving an entire prelude below
 the program it serves -- and preserving input order is what makes fixture 7
 possible: shake with every name as a root and you must get the whole table
 back, unchanged. That is the identity gate, and it separates "the parts table
 is mis-cut" from "the closure is wrong", two bugs that otherwise share a
 symptom.

 UNMATCHED ROOTS ARE REPORTED, NOT SWALLOWED. A root naming no part is either
 a real missing dependency or an over-matching extractor, and this code cannot
 tell which. It returns them and lets the caller rule. `cite_resolve.py` in
 the ladder independently arrived at the same two-output shape for the same
 reason, on a different graph.

 FIXTURE 8 DISCRIMINATES AND THE FIRST VERSION DID NOT. It was written as
 [Z, Y, X] with X depending on Y depending on Z -- where the table order and a
 dependencies-first topological order are BOTH "Z Y X", so it could not tell
 them apart and would have passed either implementation. It is now [X, Y, Z],
 where table order is "X Y Z" and topological order is "Z Y X". A fixture that
 cannot fail the thing it is named after is decoration.

Section: Types

  Part = record {
   name : Text,
   needs : List Text
  }

Section: Instrument

  mk : Text, List Text -> Part
  mk (n) (ns) = Part { name = n, needs = ns }

  contains : List Text, Text -> Boolean
  contains (xs) (n) = contains-loop xs 0 n

  contains-loop : List Text, Integer, Text -> Boolean
  contains-loop (xs) (i) (n) =
   if i >= list-length xs then False
   else if list-at xs i == n then True
   else contains-loop xs (i + 1) n

 Index of the part with this name, or 0 - 1. Linear is the right shape here:
 the tables are ~100 entries and a map would need an ordering this file
 deliberately does not impose.

  find-part : List Part, Text -> Integer
  find-part (ps) (n) = find-part-loop ps 0 n

  find-part-loop : List Part, Integer, Text -> Integer
  find-part-loop (ps) (i) (n) =
   if i >= list-length ps then 0 - 1
   else if (list-at ps i).name == n then i
   else find-part-loop ps (i + 1) n

 The closure. `work` is a queue that only ever grows, `i` walks it, and `acc`
 is the reached set. A name already in `acc` is skipped, so a cycle
 terminates; `work` grows only when a name is reached for the FIRST time, so
 it grows at most once per part and the walk is finite.

 Lists are copied with `&`, never `list-push`: push MUTATES its argument and
 returns the same object, so pushing onto a list the caller still holds
 changes it for everyone.

  reach : List Part, List Text, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  reach (ps) (work) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length work then acc
   else let n = list-at work i
   in if contains acc n then reach ps work (i + 1) acc
      else let acc2 = acc & [n]
      in let j = find-part ps n
      in if j < 0 then reach ps work (i + 1) acc2
         else reach ps (work & ((list-at ps j).needs)) (i + 1) acc2

  kept : List Part, List Text -> List Text
  kept (ps) (roots) = kept-loop ps 0 (reach ps roots 0 []) []

  kept-loop : List Part, Integer, List Text, List Text -> List Text
  kept-loop (ps) (i) (acc) (out) =
   if i >= list-length ps then out
   else let p = list-at ps i
   in if contains acc (p.name) then kept-loop ps (i + 1) acc (out & [p.name])
      else kept-loop ps (i + 1) acc out

  unmatched : List Part, List Text -> List Text
  unmatched (ps) (roots) = unmatched-loop ps roots 0 []

  unmatched-loop : List Part, List Text, Integer, List Text -> List Text
  unmatched-loop (ps) (roots) (i) (out) =
   if i >= list-length roots then out
   else let r = list-at roots i
   in if find-part ps r < 0 then unmatched-loop ps roots (i + 1) (out & [r])
      else unmatched-loop ps roots (i + 1) out

  join : List Text -> Text
  join (xs) = if list-length xs == 0 then "(none)" else join-loop xs 0 ""

  join-loop : List Text, Integer, Text -> Text
  join-loop (xs) (i) (acc) =
   if i >= list-length xs then acc
   else if i == 0 then join-loop xs (i + 1) (list-at xs i)
   else join-loop xs (i + 1) (acc & " " & list-at xs i)

  report : Text, List Part, List Text -> Text
  report (label) (ps) (roots) =
   label & ": kept [" & join (kept ps roots) & "] unmatched [" & join (unmatched ps roots) & "]"

Section: Main

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let chain = [mk "A" ["B"], mk "B" ["C"], mk "C" []]
   in print-line-uni (report "1 chain      " chain ["A"])
   let diamond = [mk "A" ["B", "C"], mk "B" ["D"], mk "C" ["D"], mk "D" []]
   in print-line-uni (report "2 diamond   " diamond ["A"])
   let cyclic = [mk "A" ["B"], mk "B" ["A"]]
   in print-line-uni (report "3 cycle     " cyclic ["A"])
   let island = [mk "A" ["B"], mk "B" [], mk "C" ["D"], mk "D" []]
   in print-line-uni (report "4 island    " island ["A"])
   let two = [mk "A" ["B"], mk "B" []]
   in act
     print-line-uni (report "5 no roots  " two [])
     print-line-uni (report "6 unknown   " two ["nope"])
     print-line-uni (report "7 identity  " two ["A", "B"])
   end
   let ordered = [mk "X" ["Y"], mk "Y" ["Z"], mk "Z" []]
   in print-line-uni (report "8 order kept" ordered ["X"])
   let dup = [mk "A" ["B"], mk "B" []]
   in print-line-uni (report "9 dup roots " dup ["A", "A", "B"])
  end
